  {
    "objectID": "qualitative/content-analysis.html",
    "href": "qualitative/content-analysis.html",
    "title": "Qualitative Content Analysis",
    "section": "",
    "text": "Braun and Clarke (2021) describe QCA as the analytical approach most similar to TA:\n\nAt the general level, there does seem to be little that separates TA and QCA.\n\nQCA overlaps particularly with Coding Reliability TA and Codebook TA, while differing more substantially from Reflexive TA.\n\n\n\nConceptual overlap between QCA and TA, based on Braun and Clarke (2021).",
    "crumbs": [
      "Qualitative Research Methods",
      "Qualitative Content Analysis"
    ]
  },
  {
    "objectID": "qualitative/content-analysis.html#qualitative-content-analysis",
    "href": "qualitative/content-analysis.html#qualitative-content-analysis",
    "title": "Qualitative Content Analysis",
    "section": "",
    "text": "Braun and Clarke (2021) describe QCA as the analytical approach most similar to TA:\n\nAt the general level, there does seem to be little that separates TA and QCA.\n\nQCA overlaps particularly with Coding Reliability TA and Codebook TA, while differing more substantially from Reflexive TA.\n\n\n\nConceptual overlap between QCA and TA, based on Braun and Clarke (2021).",
    "crumbs": [
      "Qualitative Research Methods",
      "Qualitative Content Analysis"
    ]
  },
  {
    "objectID": "qualitative/content-analysis.html#approaches-to-qualitative-content-analysis",
    "href": "qualitative/content-analysis.html#approaches-to-qualitative-content-analysis",
    "title": "Qualitative Content Analysis",
    "section": "Approaches to Qualitative Content Analysis",
    "text": "Approaches to Qualitative Content Analysis\nHsieh and Shannon (2005) distinguish three approaches to QCA.\n\n\n\nThree approaches to QCA, based on Hsieh and Shannon (2005).\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nApproach\nDescription\n\n\n\n\nConventional Content Analysis\nCodes are developed inductively from the data.\n\n\nDirected Content Analysis\nInitial codes are derived from existing theories or previous research.\n\n\nSummative Content Analysis\nSelected words or content are counted and compared, followed by contextual interpretation.",
    "crumbs": [
      "Qualitative Research Methods",
      "Qualitative Content Analysis"
    ]
  },
  {
    "objectID": "qualitative/content-analysis.html#analytical-outputs",
    "href": "qualitative/content-analysis.html#analytical-outputs",
    "title": "Qualitative Content Analysis",
    "section": "Analytical Outputs",
    "text": "Analytical Outputs\nQCA can generate categories and themes at different levels of abstraction and interpretation (Graneheim and Lundman 2004; Graneheim et al. 2017).\n\n\n\nLevels of abstraction in QCA, based on Graneheim et al. (2017).\n\n\n\n\n\nOutput\nFocus\n\n\n\n\nCategories\nShared characteristics of the content\n\n\nDescriptive themes\nPatterns across the data\n\n\nThemes of meaning\nUnderlying meanings of the data",
    "crumbs": [
      "Qualitative Research Methods",
      "Qualitative Content Analysis"
    ]
  },
  {
    "objectID": "qualitative/content-analysis.html#intercoder-reliability",
    "href": "qualitative/content-analysis.html#intercoder-reliability",
    "title": "Qualitative Content Analysis",
    "section": "Intercoder Reliability",
    "text": "Intercoder Reliability\nIntercoder reliability can be assessed in QCA when multiple coders independently apply a shared coding scheme (Burla et al. 2008).\nIntercoder reliability is not required in all approaches to QCA.",
    "crumbs": [
      "Qualitative Research Methods",
      "Qualitative Content Analysis"
    ]
  },
  {
    "objectID": "qualitative/content-analysis.html#references",
    "href": "qualitative/content-analysis.html#references",
    "title": "Qualitative Content Analysis",
    "section": "References",
    "text": "References\n\n\nBraun, Virginia, and Victoria Clarke. 2021. “Can i Use TA? Should i Use TA? Should i Not Use TA? Comparing Reflexive Thematic Analysis and Other Pattern-Based Qualitative Analytic Approaches.” Counselling and Psychotherapy Research 21 (1): 37–47. https://doi.org/10.1002/capr.12360.\n\n\nBurla, Laila, Brigitte Knierim, Jürgen Barth, Katharina Liewald, Margreet Duetz, and Thomas Abel. 2008. “From Text to Codings: Intercoder Reliability Assessment in Qualitative Content Analysis.” Nursing Research 57 (2): 113–17. https://doi.org/10.1097/01.NNR.0000313482.33917.7d.\n\n\nGraneheim, Ulla H., Britt-Marie Lindgren, and Berit Lundman. 2017. “Methodological Challenges in Qualitative Content Analysis: A Discussion Paper.” Nurse Education Today 56: 29–34. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.nedt.2017.06.002.\n\n\nGraneheim, Ulla H., and Berit Lundman. 2004. “Qualitative Content Analysis in Nursing Research: Concepts, Procedures and Measures to Achieve Trustworthiness.” Nurse Education Today 24 (2): 105–12. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.nedt.2003.10.001.\n\n\nHsieh, Hsiu-Fang, and Sarah E. Shannon. 2005. “Three Approaches to Qualitative Content Analysis.” Qualitative Health Research 15 (9): 1277–88. https://doi.org/10.1177/1049732305276687.",
    "crumbs": [
      "Qualitative Research Methods",
      "Qualitative Content Analysis"
    ]
  },
